robco-term (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * tmux -CC attach survives a window whose screen holds an OSC 8
    hyperlink. The control-mode envelope closes only on the ESC \ that
    follows tmux's %exit line, so a hyperlink's terminator inside a
    capture-pane reply is payload. The first such window used to end the
    attachment, and the rest of the attach scrolled onto the shell.
  * A layout change for a window that is not current, which tmux sends
    with an empty flags field, parses instead of logging a malformed
    notification per window on every attach.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 22:35:41 +1000

robco-term (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The cabinet is the window's handle. Drag the bezel, the margin around
    the text, or the casting beside the channel bank, and the window
    moves; a drag on the text selects, as before.
  * Every chord is a row in a [bindings] table in the config file, in
    Rio's syntax: a row on a shipped trigger replaces it, a new trigger
    joins. Ctrl+Shift+B folds the channel bank away and brings it back.
  * Ctrl+click (Cmd+click on macOS) opens the link under the pointer, an
    OSC 8 hyperlink or a URL in the text. While the chord is held the
    link is underlined and the pointer is a hand; a press that travels
    selects the link's text instead.
  * A second face for prose. --prose-font names a proportional family,
    and a row with nothing to line up is set in it, each character at
    its own width; rows that match screen.monospace_trigger (box
    drawing, bars, rules, a run of spaces after text) keep the
    configured face. screen.prose_scaling sizes the prose face against
    it. --font picks the configured face for the life of the process.
  * The wheel scrolls a pager or an editor on the alternate screen: it
    sends the arrow keys, three lines to the notch.
  * A paste cannot write the escape that ends a paste. Clipboard text
    carrying the end-of-paste marker closed the bracket early, and a
    shell took the rest as typing.
  * The cursor stays visible inside a selection, on a reverse-video
    cell, and on a light plate: it takes whichever of its colours stands
    farther from the plate under it, and is redrawn with its row.
  * A hovered link on text the program had already underlined shows its
    line.
  * Choosing a wider system font in the settings window no longer ends
    the process: the window's floor settles before its boundary.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 21:46:25 +1000

robco-term (0.2.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Critters come on the clock. One crosses the glass at the start of
    each interval counted from midnight, so at the shipped quarter hour
    you get one at :00, :15, :30 and :45, and catching one tells you the
    time without looking. Setting `timing` to `random` keeps the same
    interval and puts the arrival at an unpredictable point inside it.
  * A critter no longer greets you whenever you come back to the window.
    A window counts as unattended the moment it loses the keyboard, and
    an arrival that fell due while nobody was there used to wait for the
    return, so returning was the commonest way to see one. An interval
    nobody was watching now brings nothing.
  * An empty bank tunes to no-signal: the air lands on a rowless bank and
    the glass carries the casting with a centred NO_SIGNAL notice.
    Ctrl+Shift+T there opens slot 01, and a returning gateway whose berth
    is taken lands on the next free slot.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 16:34:45 +1000

robco-term (0.2.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A second window can close without taking the process down with it.
    Every window shared one GPU instance created above them; opening a
    second window and closing the first crashed on NVIDIA's Wayland
    driver, which reaches into state a sibling instance had already
    freed. The instance is now made once, above every window, and handed
    down rather than remade per window.
  * A selection clears when you leave the channel it was made on, rather
    than waiting there for you. Parking it on the row and handing it back
    on return was only safe while the arriving channel's history stayed
    short of its scrollback: the mark's row number decays as the ring
    evicts, and a mark in the wrong place looks exactly like one in the
    right place. Nothing marked is lost, since the middle button already
    pastes what was copied on any channel.
  * A tmux -CC channel's selection stops drifting once its scrollback
    fills. Konsole's model names a row by an index that shifts as history
    evicts old lines, and a drag spanning an eviction used to hand the
    wrong rows to the middle button; the model now rebases its rows
    against the grid's own eviction count on every gesture and every
    frame.
  * The pointer's memory of the cell it was last over, and the trackpad
    travel it was banking toward a wheel notch, now clear with the
    channel switch alongside the gesture they belonged to. A first motion
    that landed on the same cell the old channel left behind used to read
    as no movement at all.
  * The pager's prev and next thirds take a mouse press the way Alt+PageUp
    already does, and a press that crosses into another bank lands on
    that bank's remembered channel.
  * A bank remembers the channel it last had on the air. Paging onto
    another bank's stretch switches the glass to that bank's remembered
    channel; paging within the same bank stays view-only, and a bank
    whose remembered channel went dark comes back on its first open row.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 20:30:00 +1000

robco-term (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The screen stops when you do. A window that has lost the keyboard, or
    has gone five minutes without a keystroke, click or scroll, holds the
    picture it has: the flicker, the sweep, the noise and the jitter stop,
    the whole shader chain goes quiet, and a critter waits rather than
    crossing a screen nobody is watching. Whatever the shell says still
    arrives and still shows, and a keystroke or a click brings the tube
    back at once. This replaces the old half-rate and quarter-rate steps,
    so `unfocused_frame_skip` and `idle_frame_skip` are gone;
    `effects_frame_skip` is now the one cadence, and it is what the glass
    runs at whenever you are there.
  * A channel keeps what was yours on it. The screen comes back whole
    instead of showing the last channel's picture under the new channel's
    cursor, a run you marked with the mouse is waiting where you left it,
    and arrow keys and Return now follow the modes the program on that
    channel actually set. A half-typed input-method composition and a
    drag in progress are dropped at the switch rather than landing on the
    channel you moved to.
  * The find line comes down when you leave the channel it stands on, and
    opens again with the last thing you looked for already in it. It used
    to stay behind and take no keys but its own channel's, so the chord
    did nothing on any other channel and nothing at all once the channel
    it was raised on had been closed.
  * Alt reaches the shell the way readline's Meta does. Alt+. , Alt+f and
    Alt+Backspace used to arrive as a bare key with no escape in front of
    it, so the last argument, the word ahead and the word behind the
    cursor were unreachable. The Mac keeps its own Option, which composes
    a character rather than standing for Meta.
  * A digit chord switches channels whether or not a bank is drawn. It
    used to ask the mouse's own display and stand down when nothing was
    drawn, so a set configured with no chassis had no keyboard route to
    its channels at all.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 12:10:00 +1000

robco-term (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A destination can be an IPv6 address. `--ssh 2001:db8::1` goes where
    it says, and the brackets carry a port the way `ssh` spells one,
    `--ssh [2001:db8::1]:2222`. Before this the last group of the address
    was read as a port number and what was left went to the resolver,
    which had never heard of it.
  * The Serial tab says what the rate does not reach. A shell reached over
    SSH, and a tmux window under `tmux -CC`, have had their output read off
    a transport before this terminal holds it, so neither is slowed. The
    note sits under the tab's own rows, where somebody turning the switch
    on is looking.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 23:39:38 +1000

robco-term (0.1.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The terminal will run on a slow line if you ask it to. Switch on
    `[serial]` and a local shell's output arrives at the baud rate you
    name, 19200 by default, which fills an 80 by 25 screen in about a
    second. It is the reading that is slowed and not the drawing, so a
    program blocks when the buffer between you fills, exactly as it would
    have behind a real wire, and `Ctrl`+`C` stops the output because it
    stops the program writing it. A screen copied or searched holds what
    the screen holds. A shell reached over SSH and a tmux window are not
    slowed. The settings window has a Serial tab for both keys.
  * The glass slows down when nobody is watching it. Clicking away to
    another window halves the rate the CRT animates at, and half a minute
    without a keystroke halves it again; a keystroke or a click brings it
    straight back. The steps multiply whatever `effects_frame_skip` is
    set to, so a screen tuned to run fast still runs faster than a
    default one does. Set `unfocused_frame_skip` or `idle_frame_skip` to
    1 to switch either step off.
  * The bank's records are built once and kept, instead of being rebuilt
    from the pieces on every frame to draw the same picture.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 18:15:00 +1000

robco-term (0.1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A click lands on the row it is over. The pointer measured the glass
    against the window rather than against the picture drawn on it, so a
    click near the top of the screen was right and one further down took
    a row above the one under the cursor, by more the lower it fell and
    by more the wider the screen margin was set.
  * The bank costs about a tenth less to keep on screen. It was
    rasterised and uploaded to the graphics card again on every frame,
    twenty times a second, to put back the pixels already there; it is
    now built again only when a channel changes or the window is
    resized.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:22:50 +1000

robco-term (0.1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every so often something drawn walks across the glass and leaves. A
    whale spouts, three ducks paddle past, the D51 runs through. It is
    not a screen saver: it comes while you are typing, a keystroke does
    not chase it away, and it is drawn over the terminal's picture rather
    than into it, so text scrolls behind one and a selection copied
    across one still gives back what the session wrote. Every piece is
    off any cell it touches within about a second.
  * The Critters tab settles whether they visit, how long on average
    between one and the next, and which of the eight are still in the
    cast. The interval is an average and not a period, so one is never
    due.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 16:02:19 +1000

robco-term (0.1.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The catalogue's wide 16-pixel face is GNU Unifont, under the SIL Open
    Font License, covering the whole printable Basic Multilingual Plane
    and the emoji planes above it. The Unscii 16 it replaces carried a
    GPL-derived file this package could not distribute under its own MIT
    terms.
  * A face whose catalogue row names a fallback now reaches it: the
    bundled faces answer an uncovered character before the machine's own
    fonts are read, so a bare container and a loaded desktop draw the
    same glyph.

